Application Prompting Summary
1. The 7-Section Prompt Template
Instead of writing unstructured natural language, professional prompts for applications follow a robust structural framework:

Purpose: Defines the primary goal and outcome of the prompt using direct, action-oriented language.

Input Description: Clarifies the structure, expected schema, and quirks (like slang or errors) of the incoming data.

Output Description: Outlines format specifications, required schema structures, content rules, and negative constraints (e.g., returning valid JSON only).

Success Criteria: Details what a high-quality, accurate, and consistent result looks like.

Instructions: (Optional) Explicit step-by-step procedures used only when the task flow isn't self-evident.

Background: Provides critical context regarding who will consume the output and how the downstream pipeline uses it.

Examples: Strategic few-shot demonstrations showing the model how to handle edge cases, tone shifting, and complex parsing.

2. Core Principles of Prompt Engineering
Clarity: Use precise verbs and unambiguous definitions to prevent the model from guessing.

Completeness: Provide all necessary schema and formatting context upfront so the model doesn't improvise blindly.

Organization: Logically separate sections using clear headings, placing heavy emphasis on critical constraints at the beginning or end.

Consistency: Keep naming conventions, formats, and categorical lists uniform.

3. Operational Best Practices
Evaluation-Driven Iteration: Build baseline metrics and evaluation scripts before tweaking prompts, ensuring every change moves performance forward objectively.

Handling Template Collisions: When embedding structured code blocks like JSON into prompt templates, remember to escape internal curly braces ({{ and }}) so the template engine doesn't mistake them for variables
